# local-llamacpp-cpu-2b-q4_0 — authoritative configuration.
#
# The directory name is documentation; this file decides. Never copy a slot value
# out of the directory name into a CLI flag (NAMING.md, "The name is a claim").
#
# Committed on purpose and deliberately NOT gitignored. A real environment
# variable always wins over it: Makefiles use ?= and dotenv does not overwrite.
#
# The file is named tpu.env for consistency with every sibling. There is no TPU,
# no GPU and no cloud here.
#
# STATUS 2026-09-16: SERVING. LEVERS SWEPT, NO SERVING BENCHMARK YET.
# Until 2026-09-15 this directory was a byte-identical copy of
# local-llamacpp-1650ti-2b-q4_0. Values below that came from that rig were
# measured on a GPU; KV_CACHE_TYPE and FLASH_ATTENTION are still UNMEASURED here,
# but THREADS and THREADS_BATCH are not — see their comment.
# llama.cpp is built CPU-only at the commit recorded below and the GGUF is at
# MODEL_PATH. First light on exactly these values: a single 328-token completion
# at 24.8 tok/s end-to-end. A smoke test, NOT a serving benchmark — sweep.py has
# never run here and benchmarks/reports/ is empty.

# ── Model ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MODEL_NAME is the upstream artifact; MODEL_PATH is what llama.cpp opens. The
# GGUF lives outside the git work tree on purpose.
MODEL_NAME=google/gemma-4-E2B-it-qat-q4_0-gguf
MODEL_PATH=/home/xbill/models/gemma-4-E2B-it-qat-q4_0/gemma-4-E2B_q4_0-it.gguf

# Slot 5 of the directory name. Reads off MODEL_NAME, not off the file's
# dominant tensor type — which is Q6_K, not Q4_0. See CLAUDE.md.
QUANTIZATION=q4_0

# ── Hardware ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MEASURED-STATIC 2026-09-15, lscpu + /proc/meminfo + /sys/devices/cpu_{core,atom}.
# No /dev/nvidia* exists on this host.
ACCELERATOR_TYPE=cpu
CPU_MODEL="13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-1360P"
CPU_CORES=12
CPU_THREADS=16
# Hybrid: 4 P-cores with SMT (logical 0-7) + 8 E-cores (logical 8-15).
CPU_P_CORE_LOGICAL=0-7
CPU_E_CORE_LOGICAL=8-15
# avx avx2 avx_vnni. NO avx512 of any kind.
CPU_SIMD="avx2 avx_vnni"
HOST_RAM_GIB=15
TENSOR_PARALLEL_SIZE=1

# ── Runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A CPU-only build: GGML_CUDA=OFF. `make build` produces it. It is a separate
# build directory from any GPU build on purpose — start_model_server refuses a
# binary that has a GPU backend next to it or linked into it.
LLAMA_CPP_DIR=/home/xbill/llama.cpp
LLAMA_CPP_COMMIT=82324fc50
LLAMA_SERVER_BIN=/home/xbill/llama.cpp/build-cpu/bin/llama-server

# ── Serving ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The endpoint is not discovered; it is known before the process starts.
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=8080
ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:8080

# NOT configurable. `-ngl 0` and an empty CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES are hardcoded in
# both `make serve` and server.py, because a CPU rig that can be flipped onto a
# GPU by one env line measures whichever device happened to be present.

# 18 KiB/token geometry (MODELS.md), but llama.cpp caps the 12 sliding layers at
# the 1024-cell window, so KV at 8192 is ~60 MiB, not 144 (MEASURED on the 1650ti
# sibling from the allocation log). Host RAM is not the constraint at this size.
CONTEXT_SIZE=8192

# UNMEASURED ON CPU. f16 is kept because the q8_0 regression was measured on the
# GPU sibling and the KV is tiny either way; whether dequant costs the same on
# AVX2 is an open question, not a settled one.
KV_CACHE_TYPE=f16

# UNMEASURED ON CPU. The +4.8% was a CUDA result.
FLASH_ATTENTION=1

# Prometheus metrics at $ENDPOINT/metrics. Operational visibility only — never
# the benchmark decode column (see CLAUDE.md).
METRICS=1

# SWEPT 2026-09-16 (benchmarks/runs/2026-09-16-thread-sweep-cpu). Both values
# survive, but NOT for the reason written here before: thread count is a weak
# lever, not the main one. Decode spans only 22.97-25.58 t/s over -t 4/8/12/16,
# non-monotonic, and 4 is within noise of the best cell. -tb 8 is the fastest
# prefill cell (139.77 t/s) ONLY when pinned to the P-core logical threads;
# unpinned it reads 131.86 because threads leak onto the E-cores.
# The straggler reasoning below was right and is bigger than the thread lever:
# 4 P-cores prefill at 135.52, adding all 8 E-cores DROPS it to 116.77. But
# nothing in this file pins anything — see the affinity note after PARALLEL_SLOTS.
THREADS=4
THREADS_BATCH=8

# One interactive session. llama.cpp SPLITS --ctx-size ACROSS SLOTS, so raising
# this without raising CONTEXT_SIZE shrinks each sequence.
PARALLEL_SLOTS=1

# ── CPU affinity: THE LARGEST MEASURED LEVER, AND NOT A KEY ON PURPOSE ────────
# MEASURED 2026-09-16: prefill spans 86.61-139.77 t/s, a 1.61x swing decided by
# WHICH cores run it rather than by thread count. E-cores are stragglers because
# llama.cpp splits work evenly and every barrier waits on the slowest thread.
#
# A MASK MUST NEVER BECOME A KEY HERE. A hex mask is not configuration, it is a
# fact about one die: 0x55 means "one thread per P-core" only because THIS host
# is an i7-1360P with 4 SMT P-cores at logical 0-7 and 8 E-cores at 8-15. On a
# homogeneous server CPU there are no E-cores to exclude and the same constant
# pins to a quarter of the machine; on another hybrid split it pins to the wrong
# cores, silently. That is the `tpu.env`-vs-directory-name mistake in another
# costume: never freeze a hardware-derived value as config.
#
# DERIVE IT, the way cpu_status already re-reads topology from /proc and /sys:
#   prefill set = /sys/devices/cpu_core/cpus          (absent => online CPUs)
#   decode  set = lowest of each distinct
#                 /sys/.../cpu<N>/topology/thread_siblings_list within that set
#   mask        = sum(1 << cpu)
# On this host that yields 0xFF and 0x55 — the two masks measured above — and it
# degrades correctly: no cpu_core means homogeneous, and no SMT makes the sibling
# groups singletons so the decode mask equals the prefill mask.
#
# THREADS and THREADS_BATCH below have the same defect in milder form: 4 and 8 are
# |decode set| and |prefill set| on this host, frozen as integers.
#
# server.py passes no affinity flags yet, and that is still gated on evidence, not
# on design: the 1.61x came from llama-bench, which has no HTTP path, and the
# prefill win is invisible to a short prompt. Confirm end-to-end with sweep.py's
# context sweep BEFORE wiring it in — a flag being accepted is not evidence it did
# anything. llama-server spells it -C/--cpu-strict and -Cb/--cpu-strict-batch.
